<!--
  ⭐ M13 — the split that matters is SHAPE vs BEHAVIOUR, and only one of them is device-specific.

  Owner decision (2026-08-07): the phone runs LANDSCAPE and looks like the TV. So every phone now
  loads the TV layout, and the flags that describe **which arrangement a layout is in** must agree
  with that layout on BOTH devices — they are no longer a device question at all. Getting this wrong
  is visible immediately: the phone briefly had the TV home layout with the phone's horizontal nav
  bool, and the navigation bar landed in the middle of the hero.

  What stays device-specific is **how the user drives it**. A TV is a D-pad: focus moves, then
  CENTER acts. A phone is a finger: one tap acts, and there is no focused row for an affordance to
  hang off. Those four flags are overridden in `values-television`.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- ── SHAPE: matches the layout, same on both devices (the TV arrangement) ────────── -->

    <bool name="home_nav_is_horizontal">false</bool>
    <bool name="browse_categories_are_horizontal">false</bool>
    <bool name="settings_categories_are_horizontal">false</bool>
    <bool name="epg_strip_is_horizontal">true</bool>

    <!-- ── BEHAVIOUR: the phone answers here, TV overrides in values-television ────────── -->

    <!--
      Which Live screen a "live" hop opens when the user has not chosen. An explicit choice in
      Settings still wins.

      This one IS a device question, unlike the shape flags above, and it was answered wrongly for
      the phone: both defaulted to the EPG guide. A guide grid is a 10-foot instrument — hours
      across, dozens of channels down — and on a handset it swallows the screen, leaving the channel
      list unreadable, which is the whole reason someone opens Live. Owner, 2026-08-12: the phone
      opens the three-column browse.

      The TV keeps the guide: at three metres the grid is the point.
    --><bool name="live_defaults_to_classic">true</bool>

    <!--
      Does each rail item carry its own label?

      On TV, no: the focused item names itself in the shared flyout, so a label under every glyph is
      redundant at three metres. On a phone there IS no focus, so the rail was six unlabelled
      glyphs and you had to scroll a navigation bar to find out that Debrid and Settings existed.

      M13 tried labels once and reverted them: inside the 60dp rail they wrapped — "Sear/ch",
      "Movi/es". That was a width problem, not a labelling one. The rail is 76dp on a phone now and
      the label is single-line with an ellipsis, so the failure that caused the revert cannot recur.
    -->
    <bool name="nav_items_show_label">true</bool>

    <!--
      May screen code MOVE FOCUS on its own? On TV that is the interaction model — focus is
      remembered, restored after a refresh, routed by D-pad. On a touchscreen it is the opposite of
      the rulebook: a tap must act, and a poster that takes focus instead of opening is exactly why
      every card once needed two taps.
    -->
    <bool name="ui_uses_dpad_focus">false</bool>
    <!--
      May a list row take focus FROM A TOUCH? Rows stay `focusable` either way; this governs only
      focus acquired by touching. A row that grabs focus on ACTION_DOWN can have its click cancelled
      when the list scrolls the newly-focused row into view.
    -->
    <bool name="rows_focusable_in_touch_mode">false</bool>
    <!--
      The play circle on a source row. On TV it is a FOCUS affordance — it appears on the focused
      row and marks where CENTER will act. A touch device has no focused row, so the same rule would
      hide it forever; there it is a permanent tap affordance.
    -->
    <bool name="source_row_play_always_visible">true</bool>
    <!--
      Tapping the dimmed area behind a sheet closes it. TV leaves this off on purpose: there is
      nothing to tap, and a clickable backdrop joins the D-pad focus order as a dead stop. BACK
      already closes the panel there.
    -->
    <bool name="dismiss_on_backdrop_tap">true</bool>
    <!--
      M15. Home's own top-right strip — clock, DEVICE KEY, IPTV badge, expiry chip. A television has
      no system status bar, so the app draws its own; a phone already has one, and the app's clock
      simply duplicates the system clock a few hundred pixels away.

      It also actively broke the hero: the strip occupies x 372..882dp, the hero text column runs to
      476dp, and on a 411dp-tall screen a two-line featured title is pushed up into it — measured,
      "|AR| WC PSG VS Real Madrid Semi Final" rendered straight through the KEY chip. Insetting the
      hero below the strip does not fit either: it needs a 280dp hero section, which pushes Continue
      Watching off the bottom.

      Nothing is lost on the phone: the device key and the expiry date are both on the Settings
      screen (Account / About), and the expiry warning still arrives as a dialog at launch.
    -->
    <bool name="home_shows_tv_status_strip">false</bool>
    <!--
      M16. The detail page's "Because you watched" rail is pinned to the bottom of the screen, and
      on a 411dp-tall handset it takes 169dp of it. What is left for the film's own column is 158dp
      — not enough for the title, the metadata, the plot AND the Watch Now button, so the primary
      action ended up below the fold. §1c's first rule is that a primary action is never
      unreachable, and a recommendation rail is the one thing on this page that can go: its content
      is reachable from browse and search, and this flag turns it back on in one line.
    -->
    <bool name="detail_shows_similar_row">false</bool>
    <!--
      M18. The guide's detail-panel button ("Add to Favorites" / "Set Reminder") is a STUB on both
      devices — its click shows a Toast of its own label and nothing else. At the phone's 1.6x font
      scale the fixed 176dp info block overflows and pushes this button out of its own panel anyway,
      so on a phone it is hidden outright: a dead control that is also clipped is the worst of both.
      The TV keeps it — it is part of that design and reachable by D-pad.
    -->
    <bool name="epg_shows_primary_btn">false</bool>
    <!--
      Owner decision (2026-08-10, from a photo of his real handset): the Debrid home's hero
      takes ~70% of a 411dp-tall screen and leaves ONE content row peeking at the bottom edge.
      On a phone the hero is gone entirely and the rows own the page; the TV keeps its hero.
    -->
    <bool name="debrid_home_shows_hero">false</bool>
</resources>
